# Cloudflare `_headers` — security headers for the static export.
#
# Files in `public/` are copied to the export root by `next build`, so this
# ships as `out/_headers`, which is where Cloudflare Pages (and Workers static
# assets) expect it. Nothing in the app reads this file; it is edge config.
#
# Verified against a real build of this site: 25 HTML pages, 35 script chunks,
# zero inline <style> blocks, two self-hosted woff2 faces (Geist, Geist Mono,
# emitted by next/font under /_next/static/media/ and covered by font-src
# 'self'), 23 Open Graph PNGs (fetched by link-preview crawlers, not by any
# page, so no img-src change), no fetch/XHR/WebSocket, no workers. The only external
# references are <a href> links to vgpu.sh, which are navigations and need no
# CSP allowance.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# Next fingerprints everything under /_next/static/, so it is safe to cache
# permanently. Cloudflare will not infer this on its own for a static export.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why script-src carries 'unsafe-inline'
#
# Next's App Router emits two inline <script> blocks per page carrying the RSC
# flight payload (`self.__next_f.push(...)`). Their content differs per page and
# changes on every build, so hashing them is not maintainable by hand. The
# standard fix is a per-request nonce, which requires a server — and this site
# sets `output: 'export'`, so there is no request to hang a nonce off.
#
# This is a real weakening, recorded rather than hidden. It is acceptable here
# because the site is fully static: no auth, no cookies, no forms, no user
# input, and nothing in localStorage at all since the site became light-only
# and its theme toggle was removed. The realistic
# impact of script injection is defacement, not account or data compromise.
# Every other directive stays locked down, including style-src.
#
# TRAP — style-src has no 'unsafe-inline', and that holds only while every StyleX
# style is static. StyleX compiles a *dynamic* style — `foo: (n) => ({ gap: n })`
# — to an inline `style` attribute carrying a CSS custom property, and this
# directive makes the browser drop that attribute. The declaration then resolves
# to nothing and the property silently falls back to its initial value. Nothing
# reports it: no console error, no failed request, and `npm run smoke` reads the
# markup rather than rendering it, so the attribute is present and the test
# passes. It is also invisible locally, where `out/` is served without headers.
#
# This was not hypothetical. `ui.tsx`'s gap helper was dynamic, and the first
# deploy carrying these headers shipped 138 collapsed gaps across six pages -
# most visibly a hero whose buttons sat flush against the command block below
# them. The fix was a static gap scale, not 'unsafe-inline'. Keep it that way:
# if a dynamic style is ever genuinely needed, it needs 'unsafe-inline' here and
# that trade should be made deliberately, not discovered in production.
#
# TRAP — do not "improve" this by adding a hash alongside 'unsafe-inline'.
# Under CSP2+, the presence of any hash or nonce makes browsers IGNORE
# 'unsafe-inline' for that directive, so adding the theme-bootstrap hash below
# would silently break all 50 flight-data scripts and blank every page.
#
#   theme bootstrap (identical on all 25 pages):
#   'sha256-Mtmu/iCN5bY/V+BTx63tyOR7/hcEYzzDRzw2SLtaFrU='
#
# Two ways to go strict later, in increasing order of effort:
#   1. A post-build script that hashes every inline script per page and emits a
#      per-route _headers block. Mechanical, but regenerates on each build and
#      pushes against Cloudflare's _headers rule limits at 25+ routes.
#   2. Serve through a Cloudflare Worker that injects a per-request nonce and
#      rewrites the CSP. Correct, and the only option that scales — but it means
#      giving up pure static hosting.
#
# HSTS is deliberately absent: set it in the Cloudflare dashboard under
# SSL/TLS -> Edge Certificates -> HSTS, where it belongs as a zone-wide policy.
# Be deliberate about `preload` — it is submitted to a browser-baked list and is
# slow and painful to reverse.
